<script server>
import report from '~/public/benchmarks/results.json'
const title = 'The declaration benchmark lab'
const description = 'Compare dtsx with TypeScript, Oxc, SWC, tsdown, tsup, Rollup, API Extractor, and unplugin tools using reproducible benchmarks.'
const pathname = '/benchmarks/'
const groups = [
  { id: 'project-emit', name: 'Per-module emission', detail: 'Declarations per source file. A semantic typecheck is not required.' },
  { id: 'checked-project', name: 'Checked compiler builds', detail: 'Includes semantic diagnostics. These compilers do extra work; compare within this group.' },
  { id: 'bundled-project', name: 'Standalone bundles', detail: 'One public declaration entry. Checking and JavaScript work vary by pipeline.' },
]
const sizes = [1, 25, 100]
const measured = report.results.filter(row => row.status === 'passed').length
</script>
<script client>
const theme = useColorMode({ attribute: 'data-theme' })
const size = state('100')
</script>
@nolayout
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>@include('head')</head>
<body>
@include('nav')
<main id="main" class="document-main shell">
  <a class="back-link" href="/">← Back to dtsx</a>
  <div class="document-heading"><h1>The numbers.<br><span>And how we got them.</span></h1><p>A fair comparison starts with a clear workload. Pick a project size and compare tools doing similar work.</p></div>
  <div class="lab-meta"><div><strong>16</strong><span>tool configurations</span></div><div><strong>3</strong><span>project sizes</span></div><div><strong>9</strong><span>samples per case</span></div><div><strong>{{ measured }}/48</strong><span>qualified cases</span></div></div>
  <div class="lab-controls"><label for="project-size">Library size</label><select id="project-size" x-model="size"><option value="1">1 module + entry</option><option value="25">25 modules + entry</option><option value="100">100 modules + entry</option></select><a class="text-link" href="/benchmarks/results.json" download>Download raw results ↓</a></div>
  @foreach(groups as group)
  <section class="lab-group" aria-labelledby="group-{{ group.id }}"><h2 id="group-{{ group.id }}">{{ group.name }}</h2><p>{{ group.detail }}</p>
    @foreach(sizes as count)
    <div :if="size() === '{{ count }}'" class="table-scroll"><table class="results-table"><caption>{{ count }} modules + one entrypoint · milliseconds · lower is faster</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Tool / version</th><th scope="col">Median</th><th scope="col">Min – max</th><th scope="col">Samples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    @foreach(report.results.filter(row => row.group === group.id && row.modules === count).sort((a, b) => (a.medianMs ?? Infinity) - (b.medianMs ?? Infinity)) as row)
    <tr class="{{ row.id.startsWith('dtsx') ? 'highlight-row' : '' }}"><th scope="row">{{ row.name }}<small>{{ row.version }}</small></th>@if(row.status === 'passed')<td class="number">{{ row.medianMs.toFixed(1) }}</td><td class="number range">{{ row.minMs.toFixed(1) }} – {{ row.maxMs.toFixed(1) }}</td><td>{{ row.samplesMs.length }} / 9</td>@else<td colspan="3" class="unavailable">Unavailable: published library is Linux x64 only</td>@endif</tr>
    @endforeach
    </tbody></table></div>
    @endforeach
  </section>
  @endforeach
  <noscript><p class="notice">All project sizes are shown with JavaScript disabled. Download the raw results for the full data.</p></noscript>
  <section id="methodology" class="methodology"><h2>What these results measure.</h2><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Same source. Fresh process.</h3><p>Each tool receives the same synthetic, annotated library. Every sample starts in a new process and a fresh output directory. Filesystem caches are warm. Incremental and result caches are not reused.</p></article><article><h3>Useful output comes first.</h3><p>Every sample must exit successfully, emit declarations, and pass a strict consumer covering every module. Two negative assignments must produce exactly TS2322. Bundled declarations are tested in a standalone directory.</p></article><article><h3>The whole command counts.</h3><p>Wall time includes startup, package loading, source reads, emission, writes, and disposal. Cleanup and correctness checks happen outside timing. Two warmups precede nine samples, with tool order rotated each round.</p></article><article><h3>Different tools do different work.</h3><p>TypeScript compilers perform semantic checks. API Extractor and Rollup include prerequisite tsc emission. Vite runs its JavaScript pipeline. tsup and tsdown use declaration-only mode. The adapter source shows every setting.</p></article></div>
  <div class="notice"><h3>One machine. A defined workload.</h3><p>Measured November 7, 2026 on an Apple M3 Pro, macOS arm64, 11 CPU cores, 18 GiB memory, Bun 1.4.4. This was a developer machine with uncontrolled background load, not dedicated benchmark hardware. Wide ranges are visible in the table and raw samples.</p><p>The fixtures exercise annotated interfaces, re-exports, generics, private class fields, mapped types, and enums. They exclude external dependencies, JSX, watch mode, declaration maps, and incremental rebuilds. The checks qualify this API; they do not prove correctness for arbitrary TypeScript.</p><p>The published Zig package is <code>@stacksjs/zig-dtsx@0.9.26</code>. It ships a Linux x64 library and no macOS library. All three unavailable cases remain in the dataset. We did not substitute a newer local workspace build.</p></div>
  <p>Oxc is the fastest per-module emitter in this snapshot. dtsx is the fastest standalone bundler among these measured configurations. Use the workload that matches your project, and rerun before choosing a tool on performance alone.</p>
  <div class="document-links"><a href="https://github.com/stacksjs/dtsx.org/tree/main/benchmarks">Inspect and rerun the suite ↗</a><a href="/benchmarks/methodology.md" download>Download the methodology ↓</a><a href="https://github.com/stacksjs/dtsx.org/actions/workflows/benchmarks.yml">Run the Linux workflow ↗</a></div>
  </section>
</main>
@include('footer')
</body>
</html>
